<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- POGIL activity, Day 2: reading shape of f from the graph of f' -->
<!-- Continues the handout started last class (Day1_Activity_DerivativeDescribeFunctionBehavior) -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-derivative-graph-shape">
  <title>What Does the Graph of the Rate Tell Us?</title>

  <objectives xml:id="obj-derivative-graph-shape">
    <ul>
      <li><p>Use the graph of <m>f'(x)</m> to build a sign-analysis number line, and use it to determine the intervals where <m>f(x)</m> is increasing and decreasing.</p></li>
      <li><p>Use the <em>slope</em> of the graph of <m>f'(x)</m> to determine the sign of <m>f''(x)</m>, including recognizing an interval where <m>f(x)</m> has no concavity at all.</p></li>
      <li><p>Combine both sign analyses to sketch the correct shape of a graph of <m>f(x)</m>, and identify what information about <m>f(x)</m> is <em>not</em> determined by the graph of <m>f'(x)</m> alone.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This is our first activity together using this team-based format, though you already started
      today's work: at the end of last class, your group looked at the graph of <m>f'(x)</m> and
      worked through the review questions on the handout on your own. Today you'll work in an
      assigned team to tackle the harder question that graph raises. As with any team task, the
      goal isn't to race to an answer &#8212; it's for <em>everyone</em> in the group to be able to
      explain <em>why</em> an answer is correct, not just to have written one down.
    </p>

    <p>Each group member takes one of the following roles for the activity:</p>

    <tabular>
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell>Role</cell>
        <cell>Responsibility</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Manager</cell>
        <cell>
          Keeps the group on task and on pace; makes sure everyone has spoken before the group
          moves on; asks the instructor for help if the group is stuck after trying on its own.
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Recorder</cell>
        <cell>
          Writes the group's agreed-on answers, in the group's own words; notes any question the
          group could not resolve.
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Spokesperson</cell>
        <cell>
          Speaks for the group during whole-class discussion and can explain <em>why</em> the group
          answered as it did, not just what was written.
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Reflector</cell>
        <cell>
          Checks in periodically on whether everyone understands, not just whether the group is
          moving forward; leads a short end-of-activity check-in on what worked.
        </cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>

    <p>
      In a group of three, the Manager also takes on the Reflector's check-in duties.
    </p>

    <p>
      Last class you discussed, in general terms, what it means for a function to be increasing
      (or concave up), and how the sign of a derivative tells us this. Today you'll apply those
      ideas to a specific function &#8212; but this time all you're given is a picture, not a
      formula. Below is the same graph of <m>f'(x)</m> from last class's handout, the derivative of
      an unknown function <m>f(x)</m>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-rate-graph-day2">
      <caption>Graph of <m>y = f'(x)</m>, piecewise linear.</caption>
      <image source="images/Day1_Activity_RateGraph.png" width="70%"/>
    </figure>
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-sign-analysis-f" workspace="0in">
    <title>Where Is f Increasing or Decreasing?</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Start with what you already know: the sign of <m>f'(x)</m> tells you whether <m>f(x)</m>
        is increasing or decreasing. Here you have to read that sign straight from the picture.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-fprime-roots" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find every value of <m>x</m> where the graph of <m>f'(x)</m> crosses the <m>x</m>-axis (roots).
          List them in increasing order.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-fprime-sign-chart" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Draw a number line for <m>x</m> running from <m>-12</m> to <m>10</m>, marked with the
          values you just found. On each interval between them, record whether <m>f'(x)</m> is
          positive or negative. How do you read this off the graph without computing anything.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-f-monotonicity" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Translate your sign chart into a statement about <m>f(x)</m>: on which intervals is
          <m>f(x)</m> increasing? On which is it decreasing? At which <m>x</m>-values does
          <m>f(x)</m> have a local maximum or local minimum, and how do you know from the chart
          alone?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-fprime-checkpoint" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose two different functions, <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m>, happened to have this
          exact same derivative graph. Would your sign chart above be the same for both of them?
          Explain.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-concavity-analysis-f" workspace="0in">
    <title>Where Is f Concave Up, Concave Down, or Neither?</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        You also know that the sign of <m>f''(x)</m> tells you the concavity of <m>f(x)</m>. But
        you were not given a graph of <m>f''(x)</m> &#8212; only the graph of <m>f'(x)</m>. Discuss
        with your group: what feature of the graph <em>you already have</em> could tell you the
        sign of <m>f''(x)</m>, without ever seeing a graph of <m>f''(x)</m> itself?
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-fprime-pieces" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The graph of <m>f'(x)</m> is made of several straight-line pieces. For each piece, decide
          whether the graph is increasing (rising left-to-right), decreasing (falling left-to-right), or constant (flat). Record the
          intervals where each occurs.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-fdprime-sign-chart" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use your answer above to build a second number line for <m>x</m> from <m>-12</m> to
          <m>10</m>, this time recording the sign of <m>f''(x)</m> on each interval.
          Note any interval where <m>f''(x)</m> is <em>zero</em> the whole way across, rather than
          just at a single point.
          What is happening at the end points of the intervals?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-fdprime-flat-interval" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Focus on the interval where <m>f''(x) = 0</m> throughout. What does this tell you about
          the shape of <m>f(x)</m> on that interval? Is it concave up, concave down, or something
          else? Describe, in words, what that piece of the graph of <m>f(x)</m> must look like.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-fdprime-general-rule" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write a general rule, in your own words, that connects a feature of the graph of
          <m>f'(x)</m> to the sign of <m>f''(x)</m>, a rule you could apply to any graph of
          a derivative, not just this one.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-sketch-shape-f" workspace="0in">
    <title>Sketching the Shape of f</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Now put both sign charts together.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-composite-chart" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Combine your two number lines into a single chart marking every <m>x</m>-value where
          something changes: where <m>f</m> switches between increasing and decreasing, and where
          <m>f</m> switches concavity (or loses concavity entirely).
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-sketch-f" workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the combined chart, sketch a graph of <m>y = f(x)</m> that shows the correct shape
          on <m>-12 &lt; x &lt; 10</m>: every rise, fall, and change in bend should match your
          chart. Don't worry about labeling any values on the vertical axis.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-sketch-reflection" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Look at the sketch your group drew. Can you determine the actual <m>y</m>-coordinate of
          any point on it &#8212; including the local maximum or minimum values? Why or why not?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-sketch-bridge" workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          On the same axes (or a second copy of the same axes), sketch a <em>different</em>
          function that has this exact same graph of <m>f'(x)</m>, but that is not the same
          function <m>f(x)</m> you sketched above. Compare your two sketches: what has to be
          identical between them? What is free to be different?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
